#{[17592186519509 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519008 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519509 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519509 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519193 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519509 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519509 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186519353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519509 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519509 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519509 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186519049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519509 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f8e9e694-aced-5daf-bf38-411472cb712e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519538 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519509 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519538 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519538 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519538 #edb/ref 1071 "Is the function $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+x-6$ even, odd, or neither?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519538 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "49d7ac71-7387-5bcd-8351-170b2eb2bef2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519538 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =3(-x)^{3}+(-x)-6 \\\\  & =-3{x}^{3}-x-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne f(x),$ the function is not even. \n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(3{x}^{3}+x-6) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{3}-x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne -f(x)$, the function is not odd. \n\nTherefore, the function is neither even nor odd." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519539 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519539 #edb/ref 1069 "q-6694" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519539 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519539 #edb/ref 1071 "Is the function $f(x)=-2x-5$ even, odd, or neither?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519539 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5272ce70-1353-5fba-a94b-5e92203eee02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519539 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =-2(-x)-5 \\\\  & =2x-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne f(x),$ the function is not even. \n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(-2x-5) \\\\  & =2x+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne -f(x)$, the function is not odd. \n\nTherefore, the function is neither even nor odd." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519544 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519544 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519544 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519544 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below defines the function $f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a+b.$\n\n| **$x$** | $-123$ | $-71$ | $-29$ | $29$ | $71$ | $123$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $8$ | $a+12$ | $-4$ | $-4$ | $20$ | $b-2$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519544 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e293714-80a3-5b8a-84fe-6e44867ed437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519544 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x)$ is an even function, we must have that $f(-x)=f(x).$ \n\nUsing the fact that $f(-71)=f(71),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-71) & =f(71) \\\\ a+12 & =20 \\\\ a & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $f(-123)=f(123),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-123) & =f(123) \\\\ 8 & =b-2 \\\\ 10 & =b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=8+10=18.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519549 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519549 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72963" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519549 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519549 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/da/da6aa84a7b37d9b776f88a7e3b6eb4cf70c303c3c0c463abd313c51bfa0781a2.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be graphs of odd functions?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6c5eea4e-4b84-5cc7-8353-9e67cf3ab163" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519549 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graphs I, III, and IV do not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, they are **not** odd functions.\n\n- On the other hand, graph II does have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that graph II shows an odd function.\n\n![](images/34/348ca99660cff1d4db774d39b543aa7cb79cf0a1787f11046e9b8a4d72fd83e7.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519555 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519555 #edb/ref 1069 "q-5891" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519555 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519555 #edb/ref 1071 "The table below defines the function $f(x).$ Is the function even, odd, or neither?\n\n| **$x$** | $-99$ | $-52$ | $-8$ | $8$ | $52$ | $99$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $150$ | $70$ | $0$ | $-11$ | $-92$ | $-180$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519555 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d2fbf06-bec2-5ef7-8e27-5ab81fcb62b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519555 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain of $f$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain of $f$\n\nFirst, we have\n\n$$\nf(-99)=150\\ne f(99).\n$$\n\nThis means that $f(-x)\\ne f(x)$ at least for some values of $x$ in the domain of the function. So, the function is not even.\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\nf(-99)=150\\ne -f(99).\n$$\n\nThis means that $f(-x)\\ne -f(x)$ at least for some values of $x$ in the domain of the function. So, the function is not odd.\n\nHence, the function is neither even nor odd." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519556 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519556 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71844" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519556 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519556 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/01/019a969103cb8aca2da4e752240079e90bc1d19e47bdadedfb0a447a3a100367.png)\n\nThe figure above shows part of the graph of $y=f(x)$ which is defined on the interval $x\\in [-4,4].$ Given that $f(x)$ is even, which of the following gives the complete graph of $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519556 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ddac438-ed03-5ef8-ae58-2b427cb03c98" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519556 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the graph of an even function must be symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\nSo, we can obtain the missing part of the graph by reflecting the given part over the $y$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the complete graph of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dcb80b98841bd0c5f08b5ebc8f15abae62dfbe9a4ba0f3a922ea3e482318a96a.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519569 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519569 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110645" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519569 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519569 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/67/67e6621b71176612aa5b515830cf4ce26fc29d99345269383235ca9f3a7f0a32.png)\n\nThe figure above shows part of the graph of $y=f(x)$ which is defined on the interval $x\\in [-6,6].$ Given that $f(x)$ is odd, which of the following gives the complete graph of $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519569 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a10e83cb-86c5-591c-aa72-4d43e4109d66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519569 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\nSo, we can obtain the missing part of the graph by rotating the given part ${180}^{∘}$ over the origin. \n\nTherefore, the complete graph of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/08/0884ca2bc46d183511c4777c70d3f767ca6d5bad3c08b17ca939cf6382fd45ee.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519580 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519426 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519580 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110598" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519580 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519580 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b0/b0189edb7d5582896a6b56623fa5b33c1dc9b65ff3d8ccaf634df452a72d9e0f.png)\n\nThe figure above shows part of the graph of $y=f(x)$ which is defined on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$ Given that $f(x)$ is even, which of the following gives the complete graph of $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519580 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc5a7bfe-60f8-5841-b9f2-93c7cc229a66" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519580 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the graph of an even function must be symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\nSo, we can obtain the missing part of the graph by reflecting the given part over the $y$-axis. \n\nTherefore, the complete graph of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/22/22914a2693ec654474ca8572587386de4c35ae3feb6663bd1c915c29bbf9e5dd.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519604 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186519457 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519604 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72360" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519604 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519604 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ad/adcf36236abe7d2e691f8341857c9ef19b12821d7e952de0521d1877f510f326.png)\n\nThe figure above shows part of the graph of $y=f(x)$ which is defined on the interval $x\\in [-2,2].$ Given that $f(x)$ is odd, which of the following gives the complete graph of $y=f(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519604 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7f794d6-627b-530b-9219-5208601d87a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186519604 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\nSo, we can obtain the missing part of the graph by rotating the given part ${180}^{∘}$ over the origin. \n\nTherefore, the complete graph of the function is shown below.\n\n![](images/f0/f096c9cb18025c79f62fc1a8d700fc920e8cfb2c71040a0c03cc2da8b8346c20.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
